\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Distribution of Textual Data Sources Used in Custom Sentiment Index Construction. The bar chart displays the relative frequency of documents contributed by each source to the sentiment corpus, including Federal Reserve speeches and minutes, Securities and Exchange Commission communications, congressional hearings filtered to economically relevant committees, and global news data from the GDELT project.}}{15}{figure.caption.21}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Custom and Reference Sentiment-Based Risk Indices, 1997 Q1 -- 2024 Q4. The upper panel displays the FinBERT and VADER custom sentiment indices constructed in this thesis. The lower panel shows the reference sentiment-based benchmarks, comprising the Economic Policy Uncertainty Index (EPUI) and the Shapiro News Sentiment Index (NSI). All series are Z-score standardised. Shaded areas denote NBER recession episodes, labelled by the dot-com bubble, the Global Financial Crisis, and the COVID-19 pandemic.}}{25}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Custom and Reference Time Series-Based Risk Indices, 1997 Q1 -- 2024 Q4. The upper panel displays the Custom Risk Index and the Custom Mix Index constructed in this thesis via the hierarchical two-stage PCA procedure. The lower panel shows the reference time series-based benchmarks, comprising the STLFSI, NFCI, KCFSI, and VIX. All series are Z-score standardised. Shaded areas denote NBER recession episodes, labelled by the dot-com bubble, the Global Financial Crisis, and the COVID-19 pandemic.}}{27}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Pairwise Correlation Matrix of All Risk Indices and the Fed Shadow Rate. The matrix displays Pearson correlations between all risk indices used in the analysis, including the custom time series-based index, the custom mixed index, the custom sentiment indices (VADER and FinBERT), the Federal Reserve benchmark indices (STLFSI, NFCI, KCFSI), the VIX, the EPU Index, the Shapiro News Sentiment Index, and the Wu--Xia Federal Reserve shadow rate. Red shading indicates positive correlation and blue shading indicates negative correlation, with intensity proportional to the magnitude of the coefficient.}}{29}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Impulse response of the STLFSI to a shadow rate shock. Shaded bands represent 68\% and 90\% posterior credible intervals. Identification via Cholesky decomposition, $p = 3$ lags.}}{34}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Impulse responses of sectoral variables to a STLFSI shock. Top: Banking sector. Second: Shadow banking sector (broad). Third: Shadow banking sector (core). Bottom: Hedge fund sector. Shaded bands represent 68\% and 90\% posterior credible intervals. Identification via Cholesky decomposition, $p = 3$ lags.}}{36}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Impulse response of the VIX to a shadow rate shock. Shaded bands represent 68\% and 90\% posterior credible intervals. Identification via Cholesky decomposition, $p = 3$ lags.}}{37}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces Impulse responses of sectoral variables to a VIX shock. Top: Banking sector. Second: Shadow banking sector (broad). Third: Shadow banking sector (core). Bottom: Hedge fund sector. Shaded bands represent 68\% and 90\% posterior credible intervals. Identification via Cholesky decomposition, $p = 3$ lags.}}{38}{figure.caption.37}%
